Skills Learned Through the Portfolio Project
How the project translates into practical applications for Asset Management
1. What I learned to build
Through this project, I learned how to move from a simple list of investments to a structured portfolio analysis framework. The work started from real portfolio positions across Nasdaq, emerging markets, European equities, S&P 500 exposure, gold, robotics and Amazon. Instead of treating these assets as isolated investments, I learned to aggregate them into a single portfolio, calculate invested capital, import historical data, clean the series and transform prices into returns. This is a fundamental skill because asset management is not only about choosing securities, but about building a repeatable process that converts raw financial data into risk, return and allocation decisions.
A first major learning outcome was portfolio data engineering. I learned how to work with Excel files, organize asset-level datasets, standardize dates, calculate daily and monthly returns, and build portfolio-level return series. This required understanding the difference between raw prices, percentage returns, cumulative capital growth and indexed performance. I also learned why monthly metrics are often more appropriate for medium-term portfolio analysis: they reduce noise, allow clearer comparison with macro variables, and are suitable for strategic allocation decisions rather than short-term trading.


2. Performance and risk analysis
The project also taught me how to evaluate a portfolio from both a performance and risk perspective. I learned to construct the capital curve, calculate total return, average monthly return, monthly volatility, Sharpe-type risk-adjusted performance and maximum drawdown. More importantly, I learned that performance cannot be judged only by final capital or raw return. A portfolio can look attractive in absolute return terms but still be fragile if it suffers deep drawdowns, excessive volatility or strong dependence on one risk factor.
A key practical skill was the construction of drawdown analysis. By calculating the running peak and the percentage loss from the previous high, I learned how to measure the real pain experienced by an investor during negative market phases. This is directly relevant for asset management because portfolio managers must evaluate not only expected gains, but also the path of returns, sequence risk and the investor's ability to tolerate losses without abandoning the strategy.



3. Monte Carlo simulation and long-term projections
Another important part of the project was Monte Carlo simulation. I learned how to simulate thousands of possible future portfolio paths by bootstrapping historical monthly returns. This allowed me to move beyond a single deterministic forecast and evaluate a distribution of possible outcomes. The project included simulations over long horizons, including scenarios with monthly contributions, which is much closer to a real accumulation strategy followed by many investors.
This skill is very useful in asset management because it helps communicate uncertainty. Instead of saying that a portfolio will reach one specific future value, a professional analysis can show a range of possible outcomes: pessimistic, median and optimistic paths. This makes the investment process more realistic and supports better financial planning, risk budgeting and client communication.


